    text            : PII Detection AI service in CTMS This feature introduces enhanced AI-driven data governance in the Siebel Clinical Trials Management System (CTMS) through the configurable integration of AI services. • CTMS Guide Yes The automatic detection and reporting of Personally Identifiable Information (PII) entered into non- designated fields is enabled. This capability is designed to uphold regulatory and privacy compliance standards by preventing the inadvertent collection of PII where it is not permitted or expected. Using AI, configured via the Siebel AI framework, it is possible to continuously monitor data inputs and provide alerts to users of potential PII entries. Alerts are generated to support remediation and configurable data masking. By embedding this layer of intelligent oversight, organizations can improve data quality, strengthen patient data protections, and reduce the risk of costly compliance violations. This also aligns with industry best practices for protecting sensitive information while maintaining operational efficiency across global clinical programs. Training on this course will be available soon on Oracle University.
    fields          :
      [0] PII Detection AI service in CTMS
      [1] This feature introduces enhanced AI-driven data governance in the Siebel Clinical Trials Management System (CTMS) through the configurable integration of AI services. The automatic detection and reporting of Personally Identifiable Information (PII) entered into non- designated fields is enabled. This capability is designed to uphold regulatory and privacy compliance standards by preventing the inadvertent collection of PII where it is not permitted or expected. Using AI, configured via the Siebel AI framework, it is possible to continuously monitor data inputs and provide alerts to users of potential PII entries. Alerts are generated to support remediation and configurable data masking. By embedding this layer of intelligent oversight, organizations can improve data quality, strengthen patient data protections, and reduce the risk of costly compliance violations. This also aligns with industry best practices for protecting sensitive information while maintaining operational efficiency across global clinical programs. Training on this course will be available soon on Oracle University.
      [2] • CTMS Guide
      [3] Yes

